\import{macros}
\meta{agent-authored}{true}

\taxon{Remark}
\title{From feedback semantics to theorem questions}
\tag{ftip}
\tag{feedback}
\tag{reward}
\tag{optimization}

\p{The decomposition in \ref{ftip-000B} is a consensus interface rather than
a transcription of one algorithm. RLHF separates preferences and learned
rewards; RLVR separates verifiable outcomes from group credit; process
supervision assigns signals inside a trace; experience replay separates data
generation from repeated updates; and auxiliary representation objectives can
carry structured targets that are not scalar rewards.}

\p{It also locates two recent analytical proposals. Dynamic Gradient Gating
monitors a proposed update before optimizer application and decides whether a
rollout batch should be reused again. Its head-gradient inequality is a
conditional diagnostic, not a universal divergence certificate. NExt maps an
early checkpoint history to a predicted future parameter displacement and then
re-grounds with real RL steps. Its low-rank observations are empirical and
coordinate-dependent; they do not prove that future feedback contains no new
information.}

\p{The associated theorem programme starts small: prove when a stochastic
credit estimator is unbiased; quantify verifier false positives and false
negatives; bound off-policy error under explicit ratio and support conditions;
construct false-positive and false-negative examples for raw gradient alarms;
and prove a no-free-feedback result by exhibiting two environments with the
same observed history but different future informative events.}

\p{Later theorems can optimize the division between fresh rollouts, safe
reuse, verifier calls, checkpoint prediction, and recovery updates. Such a
result must charge every component and evaluate on an independent task law;
otherwise a reduction in “steps” is not yet a scaling law for post-training
potential.}
